---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Fact from '../components/Fact.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import GuideSources from '../components/GuideSources.astro';
import RelatedPages from '../components/RelatedPages.astro';
import Src from '../components/Src.astro';
import { articleSchema, faqSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'UK trade mark search: check a business name';
const description =
  'How to search the UK trade marks register for your business name, why a free company name can still clash, and what registering a trade mark costs.';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'If Companies House accepts my company name, can I use it freely?',
    a: 'Not necessarily. Companies House doesn\'t check trade marks. If your name is too similar to someone\'s trade mark, they can complain, and you may have to change your company name.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I have to register a trade mark?',
    a: 'No, it\'s optional. A registered trade mark makes it much easier to stop others using your brand for similar goods or services, so many businesses register their main name or logo once it\'s settled.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a trade mark class?',
    a: 'Goods and services are grouped into 45 classes. Your trade mark protects you in the classes you choose, so a bakery and a software company can use similar names in different classes.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a UK trade mark last?',
    a: '10 years, and you can renew it every 10 years for as long as you keep using it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is Right Start?',
    a: 'An option where you pay part of the fee first and get the examiner\'s report before deciding whether to continue. It costs a little more in total but lets you stop early if there\'s a problem.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' }, { name: 'UK trade mark search', href: '/trade-mark-search/' }]}
  schema={[articleSchema({ title, description, href: '/trade-mark-search/', updated: '2026-10-31' }), faqSchema(faq)]}
>
  <h1>UK trade mark search</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A company name that's free on the Companies House register can still clash with someone's trade mark. Searching the
    trade marks register takes a few minutes and can save you a forced rebrand later.
  </p>

  <h2>Why check trade marks as well as company names</h2>
  <p>
    The two registers are separate. Companies House checks your name against other company names, not against trade
    marks. If your company name is too similar to another business's trade mark, the owner can complain and you may have
    to change it. Owning a company name also doesn't give you a trade mark.
  </p>

  <h2>How to search the UK register</h2>
  <p>The Intellectual Property Office's free search lets you look up trade marks by keyword, phrase or image, by number, or by owner.<Src id="tm-search-tool" /></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the <a href="https://www.gov.uk/search-for-trademark" rel="nofollow noopener">official trade mark search</a> and choose a keyword search.</li>
    <li>Search the distinctive part of your name. For "Copper Bakery Ltd", search "copper bakery" and then "copper".</li>
    <li>Look at the classes of each result. A clash matters most where the goods or services are similar to yours.</li>
    <li>Check similar spellings and sounds, such as "Kopper", as these can conflict too.</li>
    <li>Repeat for any logo or slogan you plan to use.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Also <a href="/">check the company name</a> and the <a href="/domain-name-checker/">domains</a>, so all three line up.</p>

  <h2>Registering your own trade mark</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col">Detail</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Apply online, one class</td><td><Fact id="tm-fee-first-class" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Each additional class</td><td><Fact id="tm-fee-additional-class" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paper application, one class</td><td><Fact id="tm-fee-post" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Right Start (pay in two parts)</td><td><Fact id="tm-right-start-stage1" /> to start, then the same again to continue</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Number of classes</td><td><Fact id="tm-classes" /> (goods 1 to 34, services 35 to 45)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Examination report</td><td><Fact id="tm-exam-report" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Opposition period after publication</td><td><Fact id="tm-opposition-period" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Usual time to registration</td><td><Fact id="tm-time-to-register" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Protection lasts</td><td><Fact id="tm-duration" />, renewable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Renewal, first class</td><td><Fact id="tm-renewal-fee" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="muted small">Trade mark fees changed on 1 April 2026; the figures above are the current ones.</p>

  <h2>Choosing classes</h2>
  <p>
    Pick the classes for what you actually sell or plan to sell soon. Each extra class costs more, and registering
    classes you don't use invites challenges. Class 35 (business services) and class 41 (education and entertainment) are
    common for service businesses. The IPO's classification guidance lists what each class covers.
  </p>

  <Faq items={faq} />

  <RelatedPages hrefs={['/', '/domain-name-checker/', '/business-name-generator/', '/company-name-rules/']} />

  <GuideSources
    factIds={[
      'tm-search-tool', 'tm-fee-first-class', 'tm-fee-additional-class', 'tm-fee-post', 'tm-right-start-stage1',
      'tm-classes', 'tm-exam-report', 'tm-opposition-period', 'tm-time-to-register', 'tm-duration', 'tm-renewal-fee',
    ]}
    extra={[{ title: 'Set up a private limited company: Choose a company name - GOV.UK', url: 'https://www.gov.uk/limited-company-formation/choose-company-name' }]}
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  li {
    margin: 0.35rem 0;
  }
</style>
